```rust
#[tokio::test]
async fn deny_rule_beats_allow_rule() {
    use crate::{AllowRule, DenyRule};
    let c = ctx()
        .with_deny_rules(vec![DenyRule::tool("Read")])
        .with_allow_rules(vec![AllowRule::tool("Read")]);
    let i = interceptors(&c);
    assert!(matches!(
        i.authorize("Read", ToolEffect::ReadOnly, &json!({"path": "a"})).await,
        PermissionDecision::Deny { .. }
    ));
}
```

This is the most critical security invariant of the allow-rule feature and the only missing coverage for it. Not a showstopper (the code order makes it structurally correct), but it is the test a security auditor would look for first.

